---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import uk from "../../../../data/targets/uk.json";
import us from "../../../../data/targets/us.json";
import meta from "../../../../data/meta.json";
const standards = [uk, us];
---
<Base title="Methods and sources" current="about">
  <h1>Methods and sources</h1>
  <p class="lede">How the nutrient planner works, where its numbers come from, and what it can't tell you.</p>

  <h2>Food composition data</h2>
  <p>
    Nutrient values come from <a href="https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/">USDA FoodData Central</a> (public domain):
    the SR Legacy dataset. About 100 cheap, common, UK-available foods are hand-matched to USDA records, given
    friendly names and practical units, and switched on by default. Around 1,600 more vegetables, fruits, pulses,
    grains, breads, nuts, seeds, dairy foods, eggs, oils, herbs and spices are added automatically by rules that
    leave out meat, fish, baby foods, branded and heavily processed products; these are off by default and can be
    switched on in the planner's food list. The food list, the rules and a match report live in the repository's
    <code>data/</code> folder so anyone can check them.
  </p>
  <p class="muted small">
    Current snapshot: {meta.foodsLastUpdated ? `built ${meta.foodsLastUpdated}, ${meta.foodCount} foods` : "not built yet"}.
    The snapshot is stored in the repository so the site keeps working even if the USDA servers change, and is refreshed every three months.
  </p>

  <h2>Recommended intakes</h2>
  {standards.map((s) => (
    <section>
      <h3>{s.longName}</h3>
      <ul class="prose">
        {s.sources.map((src) => <li><a href={src.url}>{src.title}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
      <ul class="prose muted small">{s.notes.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
    </section>
  ))}
  <p>EU (EFSA) values are planned and will be added once they have been transcribed and checked against EFSA's published reference values.</p>

  <h2>How the planner chooses foods</h2>
  <p>
    The planner solves a mixed-integer programme (with the <a href="https://highs.dev/">HiGHS</a> solver, running in your browser).
    It chooses at most your chosen number of ingredients and an amount of each, in practical steps
    (10 g, half an egg, a slice of bread), to minimise the total <em>relative</em> shortfall across all
    minimum targets, so being 20% short of iron counts the same as being 20% short of protein. Upper limits
    (salt, saturated fat, tolerable upper intake levels) and your calorie range are never exceeded.
    Further options are found by forbidding most of the ingredients of earlier options.
  </p>
  <h2>Reading the charts</h2>
  <p>
    Each radar chart shows the share of each minimum target reached. The dashed ring is the target (100%);
    points outside it are above the target. Anything above 150% is drawn on the outer ring so one very high
    value doesn't squash the rest. In the overview, vitamins, minerals and amino acids are each shown as the
    average across that group with every nutrient capped at 100%, so a surplus of one can't hide a shortfall
    in another; open each group to see the individual nutrients. Upper limits (fat, saturated fat, salt and any
    safety limit that is close) are shown separately as bars, because for those, less is better.
  </p>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>US food data, UK shops.</strong> Fortification differs between countries (for example US milk usually has added vitamin D; UK milk doesn't), and brands differ. Fortified products should be checked against their labels.</li>
    <li><strong>Iodine and some other nutrients</strong> are missing from most USDA records, so they are listed as "not assessed" rather than guessed.</li>
    <li><strong>Absorption isn't modelled yet.</strong> Iron from plants is absorbed less well than iron from meat, ALA omega-3 converts poorly to EPA and DHA, and phytates reduce zinc absorption. These are planned refinements.</li>
    <li><strong>Amino acid targets</strong> use WHO adult requirements per kg of body weight and are applied to adults only.</li>
    <li><strong>Reference values are for healthy populations.</strong> If you have a medical condition, are pregnant, or are planning food for a child, check with a doctor, midwife or registered dietitian.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
